From 927f092dc7f4721ebe80a6919e40ea249a28bccf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:49:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0275 -- report where the MIDI planner first
 fell behind

plan_behind counts the times MidiIsrPlan() found its plan below the
device floor (now + 2 SB IRQs) outside a resume, and skipped the gap in
song time. That skip is audible when it lands mid-song. But the count
alone cannot say where it happened. In the lab, 5 of 65 fixed-cycles
runs read 1 and the rest 0. Every instrumented rebuild made the event
vanish, and a byte ring showed no skip inside the played music. Each
look needed a throwaway build that moved the timing it was chasing.

The status line now carries plan_behind_at=<song_ms>@<loop>/<gap>irq/
<first|later> for the first event, and stays empty while plan_behind is
0:
- song ms: where in the song;
- the main-loop tick: where in the run;
- the planner-call gap in SB IRQs: a starved planner shows ~42 or more,
  a short gap points elsewhere;
- first: whether it was the first plan after the song's MidiIsrBegin().
  A start-of-song IRQ landing between Begin and that plan shifts the
  song by one slot before its first note and skips nothing.
Any run that shows the counter then also says where. It needs no extra
build and serves both deferred MIDI transports on this series (OPL3 and
the blind MPU replay), since they share the planner.

Reporting only: the counter, the skip and the plan are unchanged. One
bool member marks the song's first plan. The first event's fields are
kept in statics.

The status buffers grow so the new field is never cut off:
- midi_fragment 320 -> 400. The isr fragment was 258 bytes at long-run
  values before this patch (8-digit event and write counts). It is 308
  with the field filled, which fits 320 with only 12 to spare. With every
  field at its type maximum it is 390 bytes, which fits 400.
- the line buffers g_last_status and the status string 640 -> 768. The
  PCM part at its type maximum is 350 bytes, plus a full 399-byte
  fragment, gives 749.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
